snake - terminal snake game
===========================

SYNOPSIS

    snake [-afn] [-s WxH] [-t MS]    play
    snake -h                         help
    snaked [options]                 the game playing itself behind
                                     everything on the X desktop

DESCRIPTION

Steer the snake to the food (<>). Each piece makes it one cell longer,
scores a point and makes it a little faster. It ends when the snake hits
a wall or itself, and is won when it fills the board. The board fills
the terminal, a cell is two columns wide, and the game waits for the
first direction key.

It looks like btop, in Habamax colours: a box with the title and the
score in the top border, the keys in the bottom border.

A change of the terminal size (a new window in dwm) pauses the game
instead of ending it. When the terminal gets smaller than the board it
says "terminal too small" until it is big enough again; r starts a new
game sized to the terminal as it is then.

AUTOPILOT

With -a, or a at any time, the game plays itself. It goes at the food
on the slant, in a staircase, never hits anything, and always wins: it
fills the whole board.

How: the way it takes is always part of a Hamiltonian cycle, a closed
path through every cell, that the whole snake lies on in order.
Following such a cycle it can never be trapped. The cycles are made of
2x2 blocks: any tree joining all the blocks gives one, going round the
tree. At each step it plans the way to the food, near the straight
line, and looks for a tree that agrees with the body and that way. If
there is one it follows that cycle, if not the one it has; either way
the food gets nearer along the cycle with every step, so it is always
eaten. On these cycles the snake can only turn every second cell, so
its staircase has steps two cells long. Late in the game, with little
room left, it winds round more, as anyone would.

A direction key takes over from the autopilot; a gives control back.
Taken over in the middle of a game, it first steers until the snake
fits a cycle again; from then on it wins, but until then that is
likely, not certain. Games it played any part of don't count for the
best score.

The cycles need both sides of the board even, so an odd side is made
one cell shorter (for you too). A full terminal takes a long time to
fill: + and - change the speed, down to no wait at all.

OPTIONS

    -a      autopilot from the start
    -s WxH  a board of W by H cells, at least 10x6 (an odd side is
            made one shorter)
    -t MS   milliseconds per step at the start (20 to 2000, default
            150); each piece of food takes 2 off, down to 60
    -f      fixed speed: no speed-up when eating
    -n      neither read nor write the best score
    -h      help

KEYS

    h j k l, arrows   left, down, up, right (two turns can wait);
                      with the autopilot on, you take over
    a                 autopilot on and off
    +, -              faster, slower (halves or doubles the time per
                      step, 0 to 2000 ms; then no speed-up when eating)
    p, space          pause and go on (a direction goes on too)
    r                 new game
    q, Ctrl-c         quit
    Ctrl-z            stop; after fg the game is paused

FILES

    ~/.system/state/snake/best   the best score, one number

It is only kept when ~/.system/state exists (snake makes the snake
directory in it, nothing more), private (0600). Delete it to start
over; -n leaves it alone. Nothing else is written, and nothing goes
over the network.

SNAKED: THE SNAKE BEHIND EVERYTHING

snaked runs the autopilot on the whole screen, for ever: when the board
is full it starts again. It is meant to run all the time, from
~/.xinitrc. Where nothing covers it, on the desktop and the lock
screen, the snake is in clear colours (Habamax green, the food
magenta); behind the text of a terminal it is very faint, just lighter
than the background, so it never gets in the way of reading.

There is no compositor and no transparency. snaked paints the desktop
(the X root window), and at every step it puts the board in the root
window's _SNAKE property (the format is in snaked(1) and snaked.c).
Programs that read it draw the snake in their own empty background, so
it seems to run behind them:

    st      behind the text of every terminal, wherever a cell's
            background is the background colour: at the prompt, in
            vim and btop too (they use the same #1c1c1c); not behind
            the cursor, a selection or other colours
    slock   on the lock screen

Both are patched for it (their patches/snake.diff in the dotfiles);
snakebg.h is the code they share, copied into each. Programs that are
not patched (browsers, mpv, Sioyek) cover it like a wallpaper.

What it costs: one step every 200 ms by default; each step snaked
paints two small squares and writes the board (one byte a cell, about
8 KB for a 1920x1080 screen at 16 pixels a cell) into the property.
Each st that is shown redraws only the one or two lines the step
touched; an st on another tag (dwm moves those off the screen) stops
listening until it is shown again. The autopilot takes under a
millisecond a step.

    snaked                   start (only one runs per display)
    snaked -s 262626 -t 300  more visible behind text, slower
    pkill snaked             stop: the snake goes, the desktop is plain

Options: -b background (1c1c1c); behind text -s snake (242424) and -f
food (2b2620); on the desktop and the lock screen -S snake (87af87)
and -F food (d787d7); colours as RRGGBB. -c a cell's size in pixels
(16); -t milliseconds per step (200).

BUILD AND INSTALL

    make
    make install        # ~/.system/bin/snake and snaked,
                        # ~/.system/share/man/man1
    make uninstall

PREFIX=... changes where it goes. game.c is the game and its
autopilot, shared by snake and snaked; snake.c is the terminal, snaked.c
the desktop.

snake is plain C89 with only libc, no curses or terminfo: the terminal
is driven with termios and VT100/xterm escape codes. The binary is
static and stripped, so it needs nothing but the kernel. It needs a
terminal with 256 colours and DEC line drawing (st, xterm, tmux); the
Linux console has only 16 colours.

snaked needs Xlib, like the suckless tools, so it is linked against the
system's libX11 and is not static: drawing on X can't do without it.
